1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:12,092
In the late nineteenth century, one question had painters, scientists, and horse breeders arguing. When a horse gallops, do all four hooves leave the ground at the same time?

2
00:00:12,292 --> 00:00:17,204
The human eye couldn't see it. Painters drew it each in their own way.

3
00:00:17,404 --> 00:00:23,522
To settle the doubt, a photographer lined up twelve cameras with threads that the horse snapped as it ran past.

4
00:00:24,222 --> 00:00:28,330
The answer was yes. And without meaning to, he opened the door to cinema.

5
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:35,990
This is the story of cinema. How still photos learned to move, to speak, and to burst with color.

6
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,028
Chapter one. Twelve cameras.

7
00:00:39,378 --> 00:00:49,247
The experiment was funded by Leland Stanford, a railroad millionaire and horse breeder. Legend says there was a bet involved, though no one could ever prove it.

8
00:00:49,447 --> 00:00:56,547
The photographer was named Eadweard Muybridge. Cameras back then were terribly slow, and it took him years to pull it off.

9
00:00:56,747 --> 00:01:04,204
In 1878, in front of journalists, a mare named Sallie Gardner ran past the twelve cameras.

10
00:01:04,404 --> 00:01:11,772
In some photos, all four legs were in the air. But tucked beneath her belly, not stretched out like in the paintings.

11
00:01:11,972 --> 00:01:20,456
Muybridge did something else: he placed the photos on a wheel and projected them one after another. The horse ran again, now on a wall.

12
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:25,773
Why do still photos look like a running horse? Because the brain fills in the gaps.

13
00:01:25,973 --> 00:01:34,324
If you see more than ten or twelve images per second, you no longer see photos: you see motion. Just like a flipbook with drawings in the corners.

14
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:39,421
There was motion now. What was missing was showing it to a full room.

15
00:01:40,271 --> 00:01:43,486
Chapter two. Thirty-three spectators.

16
00:01:43,836 --> 00:01:54,330
In the United States, Thomas Edison's team built a box with a peephole: the Kinetoscope. You dropped a coin and watched alone, leaning over, a film just seconds long.

17
00:01:54,530 --> 00:02:00,871
In Paris, a photographer named Antoine Lumière saw that box. And he went home with an idea for his sons.

18
00:02:01,071 --> 00:02:08,081
Auguste and Louis Lumière invented a device that filmed, copied, and projected: the Cinematograph.

19
00:02:08,281 --> 00:02:16,051
On December 28, 1895, they rented the basement of a Paris café. Admission was one franc.

20
00:02:16,251 --> 00:02:19,913
There were a hundred chairs. Thirty-three people showed up.

21
00:02:20,113 --> 00:02:26,900
They watched ten short films: workers leaving a factory, a baby eating, a prank on a gardener.

22
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:35,763
You've probably heard that the audience ran away screaming at an approaching train. That film wasn't in the first screening, and there is no record of panic.

23
00:02:35,963 --> 00:02:41,232
Within a few months, the invention had already reached Buenos Aires, Montevideo, and Mexico City.

24
00:02:42,132 --> 00:02:47,669
Thirty-three spectators. The smallest audience in cinema history… and the very first.

25
00:02:48,519 --> 00:02:50,573
Chapter three. The magician.

26
00:02:50,923 --> 00:02:55,880
In the audience that night was a stage magician: Georges Méliès.

27
00:02:56,080 --> 00:03:04,296
He wanted to buy the machine from the Lumières. They said no. According to legend, they told him cinema was an invention without a future.

28
00:03:04,496 --> 00:03:08,425
So he got hold of an English projector and turned it into his own camera.

29
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:16,752
As he told it, one day he was filming a square when the camera jammed for a minute. When projected, an omnibus suddenly turned into a hearse.

30
00:03:16,952 --> 00:03:23,918
He had discovered a trick: if you stop the camera, change something, and keep filming, on screen it looks like magic.

31
00:03:24,118 --> 00:03:33,540
With tricks like that he made people vanish, multiplied heads, and in 1902, sent a spacecraft to the Moon. It lands right in its eye.

32
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:42,448
It was so successful that in the United States they copied it without permission. One distributor even offered to sell Méliès… his own film.

33
00:03:42,648 --> 00:03:50,328
Over time, audiences grew tired of his tricks and the war ruined him. He ended up selling toys and candy at a Paris train station.

34
00:03:50,528 --> 00:03:59,146
Journalists rediscovered him. In 1931 he received the Legion of Honor, presented by Louis Lumière himself.

35
00:04:00,046 --> 00:04:06,119
The Lumières filmed reality. Méliès filmed dreams. Cinema needed them both.

36
00:04:06,969 --> 00:04:08,934
Chapter four. Hollywood.

37
00:04:09,284 --> 00:04:18,036
In the United States, the business belonged to a select few. Edison and other companies formed a trust that controlled cameras, film stock, and theaters.

38
00:04:18,236 --> 00:04:22,880
Many producers moved west, to a small California town called Hollywood.

39
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:31,832
Why there? Because of the sun: you could film almost all year round. And the ocean, mountains, and desert were all close by.

40
00:04:32,032 --> 00:04:43,955
Legend says they were also fleeing Edison's lawyers, and could slip across to Mexico from there. Historians say it's more myth than fact: even the trust's own companies filmed in California.

41
00:04:44,155 --> 00:04:51,701
Movies were silent, yet almost never quiet: theaters usually had a live pianist or an orchestra playing along.

42
00:04:52,601 --> 00:04:56,397
Hollywood wasn't born because of lawyers. It was born because of light.

43
00:04:57,247 --> 00:05:00,641
Chapter five. The movies talk.

44
00:05:00,991 --> 00:05:13,449
In October 1927, The Jazz Singer premiered. It was mostly silent, with title cards. But in a few scenes the star sang and spoke… and the audience heard him.

45
00:05:13,649 --> 00:05:20,169
It was the first feature film with synchronized dialogue. Within a few years, silent cinema was over.

46
00:05:20,619 --> 00:05:28,254
Where did the sound go? At first, on a separate disc, like a vinyl record, spinning in sync with the projector.

47
00:05:28,454 --> 00:05:36,135
Later they put it right on the film strip: a narrow track beside the images. There, sound is a shadow that changes shape.

48
00:05:36,335 --> 00:05:41,783
A beam of light shines through it, a sensor reads it… and it turns back into sound.

49
00:05:41,983 --> 00:05:50,779
So it wouldn't sound strange, the film had to run at a constant speed. Twenty-four frames per second became the standard. We still use it today.

50
00:05:51,679 --> 00:05:56,502
Twenty-four frames per second were chosen so the human voice wouldn't go out of tune.

51
00:05:57,352 --> 00:06:00,835
Chapter six. Color and giant screen.

52
00:06:01,185 --> 00:06:07,035
Color arrived slowly. Méliès was already painting some of his films by hand, frame by frame.

53
00:06:07,485 --> 00:06:16,013
The real breakthrough came with three colors: red, green, and blue. Mixing those three lights creates almost any color.

54
00:06:16,213 --> 00:06:24,162
In the 1930s, a camera filmed each color onto a separate strip of film, then combined them. It was called Technicolor.

55
00:06:24,362 --> 00:06:32,042
In 1939, The Wizard of Oz began in sepia. When a door opened, the world exploded into color.

56
00:06:32,242 --> 00:06:39,030
In the 1950s, television stole audiences away from movie theaters. The answer was to do what a TV set couldn't.

57
00:06:39,230 --> 00:06:44,231
Huge, wider screens, and even 3D movies with special glasses.

58
00:06:44,431 --> 00:06:53,138
And in the 1970s, with Jaws and Star Wars, the summer blockbuster was born. Lines wrapped all the way around the block.

59
00:06:54,038 --> 00:06:58,638
Every time a rival screen appeared, cinema got bigger.

60
00:06:59,488 --> 00:07:02,524
Chapter seven. Twenty-four per second.

61
00:07:02,874 --> 00:07:17,521
In the 1990s, the computer stepped in. In 1993, the dinosaurs of Jurassic Park looked real. And in 1995, Toy Story became the first feature-length film made entirely on a computer.

62
00:07:17,721 --> 00:07:23,079
Then even the film strip disappeared: today, almost everything is shot and projected digitally.

63
00:07:23,279 --> 00:07:28,503
And the highest-grossing movies in history now make nearly three billion dollars.

64
00:07:28,703 --> 00:07:38,661
Yet every movie you watch—in a theater, on TV, or on your phone—is still the exact same thing: still images, one after another. Twenty-four per second.

65
00:07:38,861 --> 00:07:50,114
Your brain does the rest, just like with that horse. And cinema is still looking for the same thing as in that Paris basement: for a group of strangers, in the dark, to watch the same light.

66
00:07:51,064 --> 00:07:54,949
Cinema is still a horse that, for a single instant, flies.

67
00:07:55,899 --> 00:08:01,168
And now we're reading your comments: what story would you like to see in the next episode?
